xnu 0315: the clock the phone already has, and a way to set it

The port boots with the calendar at the epoch: there is no IORTC service, so
IOKitInitializeTime() only had the host's `mondriantime=` word to go on, and
nothing passed it. The board does have a clock -- the PMK8350 RTC at
rtc@6100, readable over SPMI since the port measured 467613 s there once --
but its value is 1975 because nothing has ever set it.

mondrian_spmi.c now maps the arbiter on first use (the clock asks before the
root mount, and the probe used to be the only mapper), reads the RTC through
the observer window with a carry re-read, and writes it with the same
channel-window byte transaction the SDAM restart reason has used for every
reboot. The write refuses a peripheral the AP does not own and an armed
alarm, so the only case it changes the PMIC is a clock that is the AP's to
set. The clock takes the RTC when it is a plausible date (2020..2100) and
`mondrianrtc=0` keeps the old behaviour; `mondriantime=` still wins.

"rtc" on the USB channel prints the raw seconds and "rtc set SECONDS" writes
them and moves the running clock, so a host with the right time fixes the
phone in one command instead of rebuilding an image.

